  "details": "<p>During the reign of Constantine I,\u00a0Italia Annonaria included the following administrative subdivisions:</p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Venetia et Histria</li>\r\n<li>Aemilia et Liguria</li>\r\n<li>Flaminia et Picenum</li>\r\n<li>Raetia</li>\r\n<li>Alpes Cottiae</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p><br />Under Theodosius I,\u00a0<span>Italia Annonaria included the following administrative subdivisions:</span></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Venetia et Histria</li>\r\n<li>Liguria</li>\r\n<li>Aemilia</li>\r\n<li>Flaminia et Picenum Annonarium</li>\r\n<li>Raetia Prima</li>\r\n<li>Raetia Secunda</li>\r\n<li>Alpes Cottiae</li>\r\n</ul>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "A Late Roman vicariate established by Constantine I with its administrative capital located at Mediolanum (Milan).",
